export const blocks = {
  "breeds/dogs/labrador-retriever.html": `
<h2>Registry measurements and the hip table</h2>
<p>The AKC snapshot and the UKC standard, revised on May 1, 2017, use the same mature size: males 22.5 to 24.5 inches and 65 to 80 pounds, females 21.5 to 23.5 inches and 55 to 70 pounds. AKC's life-expectancy line for the breed is 11 to 13 years. UKC recognized the Labrador Retriever in 1947 and traces the dog to the Saint Johns Retriever, a smaller Newfoundland brought to England on fishing boats. Gamekeepers crossed those imports with gun dogs. By the middle of the 19th century the water-resistant coat and the otter tail were already the point of the breed, and the name was the ordinary one by the late 1880s. The first recorded yellow appeared in a litter born in 1899. Chocolate dogs were noted around then and stayed less common.</p>
<p>OFA's archived hip table, breeds with at least 100 evaluations from January 1974 through December 2009, lists 208,931 Labrador evaluations, 17.6 percent scored excellent and 12.0 percent called dysplastic. That is a historical cumulative figure, useful when a seller waves a single "clear" at you, and it is old. Pair it with the parents' own reports and with VCA's puppy list: hip dysplasia, elbow dysplasia, osteochondritis dissecans, retinal dysplasia, and tricuspid valve dysplasia.</p>
<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.akc.org/breeds/labrador_retriever/index.cfm">AKC</a>, <a href="https://www.ukcdogs.com/labrador-retriever">UKC</a>, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20100210185346/http://www.offa.org/hipstatbreed.html">OFA hips</a>, <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA</a>.</p>
`,
  "commercial/dogs/labrador-retriever/best-food.html": `
<h2>Working weight on a swimmer's frame</h2>
<p>UKC's working-condition weights are 65 to 80 pounds for a male and 55 to 70 for a female, on a short dense double coat that hides a thickening waist until the ribs are hard to find. VCA ties extra weight directly to worse joint disease and asks for controlled growth while the dog is still a puppy. The bands already on this page, $40 to $75 for a budget kibble, $75 to $140 for a mid-range formula, and $140 to $260 for fresh or raw, are the monthly food bill for that frame. A swim day does not earn a second dinner. It earns the same measured meal, plus a rinse and an ear check, because VCA flags ear care specifically for Labs that swim. Treats still sit inside the calorie count. The otter tail and the chest were built to work in water, and the bowl stays sized to the UKC weight, including on the weeks the dog comes home soaked.</p>
<p>Food notes draw on <a href="https://www.ukcdogs.com/labrador-retriever">UKC</a> and <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA</a>.</p>
`,
  "commercial/dogs/labrador-retriever/best-enrichment.html": `
<h2>Retrieve, rest, and the collapse window</h2>
<p>UKC calls the Labrador an enthusiastic hunter with a good nose and a soft mouth, and VCA says the retrieving instinct shows up at home as chewing, carrying, and mouthing, with some dogs swallowing objects that were never food. Enrichment for this breed is a retrieve with a rule, a place to swim, and a nose task that ends. It is a schedule. The toy lines already printed here, free DIY, $10 to $30 basic toys, $25 to $75 interactive toys, and $20 to $50 subscription boxes, cover the objects. The design is what keeps a soft mouth from becoming a surgery.</p>
<p>A useful retrieve uses one dummy, a stop before the throw, and a delivery to hand. Ten minutes of that, then a sniff walk, matches the soft mouth the standard describes. An hour of ball launches with no stop teaches the dog to scream for the next throw and gives the mouth a reason to grab shoes, rocks, and socks when the ball disappears. VCA's note about ingesting non-food items is the evening version of that same drive. Trade the object for a known chew. Walk the yard after visitors, because a Labrador will carry off anything that still smells like a hand.</p>
<p>Water is the original job. UKC's history is retrieving from water, and the otter tail, thick at the base and tapering, never feathered, no longer than the hock, is the rudder VCA also mentions. A supervised swim in water you can wade, with an exit the dog has practiced, spends the athletic chest the standard asks for. A pool with a single steep step, or a dock the dog can jump from and then cannot climb, is a different sport. Rinse the coat afterward. The double coat is water-repellent, and the ears still need the cleaning VCA recommends for swimming Labs. Mud on the furnishings is a bath, included in the grooming habit, and it counts as part of the session.</p>
<p>The University of Minnesota Canine Genetics Lab describes exercise-induced collapse as the most common reason a young, apparently healthy Labrador collapses during exercise or excitement. The mutation is in dynamin-1, DNM1, found in 2007 on chromosome 9, inherited as an autosomal recessive. The protein works in the brain and spinal cord, repacking the vesicles that hold neurotransmitters. Quiet walking does not demand it. Intense work plus high excitement does, and the lab mentions a possible role for rising body temperature. In the populations they have tested, about 30 to 40 percent of Labradors carry one copy and about 3 to 13 percent carry two. The share moves with why the dogs were tested. Field, show, and pet dogs looked similar. More than 80 percent of dogs with two copies have at least one collapse by four years of age. A few dogs with two copies never collapse, because their days never reach that intensity.</p>
<p>Merck's February 2026 review times the episode: limb weakness, then ataxia and collapse, 5 to 20 minutes after intense exercise. The kneecap reflex may fade during the episode. Between episodes the dog looks ordinary, and recovery is rapid. Merck also says heterozygous dogs, and dogs who lack this mutation, can still collapse, so a clear DNA result rules out this particular variant and leaves other causes for a veterinarian. There is no drug in that review. The management is avoiding the intense bout. For a dog whose test says E/E, the enrichment plan is sniffing, short retrieves with long rests, and swimming that stays easy. For a dog who has never been tested, the same shape is the safer default until the paperwork arrives, because you cannot see carrier status on a shiny coat.</p>
<p>Nose work fits the "good nose" line in the UKC standard and spends the brain on a day when the body should stay cooler. Hide the dummy in grass. Ask for a sit at the door before the search. Quit while the dog still wants one more, which is how you keep the next session valuable. A puzzle bowl at dinner is the indoor version, and it also slows a breed VCA says will gain weight. The subscription box on the $20 to $50 line is optional. A dish towel, a cardboard box, and yesterday's dummy already do the job if you rotate them before the dog shreds them into swallowable pieces.</p>
<p>Collapse and retrieve notes: <a href="https://canine-genetics.umn.edu/canine-genetic-testing/dynamin-1-associated-exercise-induced-collapse">Minnesota</a>, <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/musculoskeletal-system/myopathies-in-dogs-and-cats/exercise-intolerance-and-exercise-induced-collapse-in-dogs">Merck</a>, <a href="https://www.ukcdogs.com/labrador-retriever">UKC</a>, <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA</a>.</p>
`,
  "commercial/dogs/labrador-retriever/best-habitat-size.html": `
<h2>A house for a square water dog</h2>
<p>UKC wants a Labrador that is square or only slightly longer than tall, with the elbow sitting about halfway from withers to ground. A male at the top of the AKC height, 24.5 inches, has a deep chest and an otter tail that needs room to lie out straight. The tail is a natural extension of the topline, thick and muscular at the base, and it hangs when the dog is relaxed. A crate that forces that tail against the door, or a bed shoved into a corner so the tail has to curl, fights the outline the standard is protecting. The price tiers already on this page, $50 to $150, $100 to $300, and $200 to $600 and up, are crate and setup money. Spend them on length and on a bed the tail can share, measured against a dog who may finish at 65 to 80 pounds if male or 55 to 70 if female.</p>
<p>VCA's home note is direct: this breed does best with access to outdoor space, and time alone is a moderate tolerance only after the dog is trained and has been exercised. A studio with a lunch-hour walk is a mismatch for the high activity line on the same handout. A fenced yard is the ordinary answer, and the fence has to assume a dog who retrieves. Balls go over fences. Labs go after balls. The gate latch belongs above a standing dog's nose, and the bottom of the fence belongs tight to the ground, because a dog bred to push into cover will find the gap that smells like the neighbor's compost. Shade matters on the days you use the yard as the exercise plan. Merck's collapse review ties intense work and possibly body heat to episodes in dogs with the dynamin-1 variant. A black coat in a yard with no shade, chasing until the dog is frantic, is the wrong use of that outdoor space.</p>
<p>Flooring is a joint decision. VCA lists hip dysplasia, elbow dysplasia, and osteochondritis dissecans in the puppy year, and weight gain later makes joint disease worse. A puppy who rockets across a bare hallway every time the door opens is practicing the slip that those elbows do not need. A runner rug on the slick path, from the crate to the door, is cheaper than any of the surgery rows on the health-costs page and it uses the same floor you already have. Stairs can stay in the house. They do not need to be the puppy's treadmill. Carry a young dog down the long flights while the growth plates are the topic of the wellness visit, and let the adult dog use the stairs once the veterinarian has seen the gait.</p>
<p>Swimming access is habitat, if you have it. UKC's coat is short, dense, and water-repellent, with a soft undercoat, and the tail carries the same thick coat, never a feathered plume. That jacket is why a Labrador can work in cold water and then stand in your kitchen. It is also why the entry and the exit have to be boringly safe. A pond with a gradual bank, a life jacket while the dog is learning, and a person in the water beat a decorative pool with a stone edge. Afterward the dog needs a place to dry that is easy to mop, because seasonal shedding, which VCA calls heavy, rides along with the damp undercoat. A mudroom hook for the towel and a weekly brush on the dry coat keep the house. Bathing lives in the "after mud or a swim" slot on the VCA handout, so the habitat includes a hose you will actually use, with water that is cool on a hot dog and warm enough in winter that you finish the rinse.</p>
<p>Ears and the sleeping spot go together. Drop ears, short and triangular, with the tip covering the eye when you pull the leather forward, trap moisture after a swim. VCA asks for ear cleaning especially in Labs that swim. The bed should let you reach the ears without a wrestling match, which means the crate door opens beside a light, and the dog is practiced at a chin rest. Put the crate in the room where the family sits in the evening. VCA's temperament line is social and eager to work with people. A dog parked in a far garage, exercised and then forgotten, spends the "moderate time alone" budget before the workday even starts. The garage can hold the food bin. The dog sleeps where the people are, on a bed long enough for a 22 to 24 inch frame and a tail that reaches toward the hock.</p>
<p>Chewing is a housing problem as much as a training one. VCA warns that the retrieving mouth will carry and sometimes swallow household objects. Cords, children's toys, and the trash live outside the crate zone, and the crate itself is how you leave the house for the errand that cannot include the dog. The supplies line on the cost page, $15 to $50 a month, is the replacement chews and the baby gate that keeps the puppy on the rug. A larger yard does not replace that gate. It adds a second place the dog can be safe while you cook. Measure the yard by whether you can see the dog from the window, and measure the crate by the dog you will have at two years, using the AKC height and weight, including a little extra length for the otter tail.</p>
<p>Housing sources: <a href="https://www.ukcdogs.com/labrador-retriever">UKC</a>, <a href="https://www.akc.org/breeds/labrador_retriever/index.cfm">AKC</a>, <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA</a>, <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/musculoskeletal-system/myopathies-in-dogs-and-cats/exercise-intolerance-and-exercise-induced-collapse-in-dogs">Merck</a>.</p>
`,
  "commercial/dogs/labrador-retriever/best-insurance.html": `
<h2>What a Labrador policy is actually asked to cover</h2>
<p>The premiums already listed on this page run about $10 to $25 a month for accident only, $30 to $80 for accident and illness, and another $10 to $25 if you add wellness. Those are the predictable numbers. The claims follow VCA's age split. In the first year the handout names hip dysplasia, elbow dysplasia, osteochondritis dissecans, retinal dysplasia, and tricuspid valve dysplasia. From one year to eight it names exercise-induced collapse, epilepsy, atopic dermatitis, progressive retinal dysplasia, gastric dilatation-volvulus, and mast cell tumors. After eight it names hemangiosarcoma and cataracts, and it tells owners to have new lumps, appetite changes, and low energy checked because cancer screening matters as Labs age.</p>
<p>OFA's hip table through December 2009, 208,931 Labrador evaluations, called 12.0 percent dysplastic and 17.6 percent excellent. A policy bought while the puppy is still unexamined is the one that can be asked to pay for the imaging and, if the dog is clinical, the surgery. A policy bought the week after a veterinarian writes "hip dysplasia" on a limp will treat that diagnosis as already known. The same timing applies to elbows and to a heart murmur that turns out to be tricuspid valve dysplasia. Accident-only coverage, the cheaper row, leaves illness off the page. Hip dysplasia, epilepsy, atopy, and the cancers on VCA's list are illness. Bloat, gastric dilatation-volvulus, is an emergency that can also be an illness with a surgical bill, and it is on the adult list. Match the row to that list before you pick the $10 plan because the monthly number looks kind.</p>
<p>Exercise-induced collapse sits in an awkward spot for insurance. Merck's 2026 review says there is no treatment and that affected dogs avoid intense activity. The University of Minnesota test tells you whether the dog is clear, a carrier, or has two copies of the DNM1 variant. A DNA test is a small line next to an orthopedic surgery. The collapse itself may produce an emergency visit the first time it happens, because a dog who goes down at the park looks like heat stroke, a heart event, or a seizure until someone who knows the breed asks about the five-to-twenty-minute window Merck describes. After the diagnosis, the cost shifts from treatment, which the review does not offer, to the life you build so the episode does not repeat. Read the policy for whether a genetic disease known before enrollment is excluded. Buying the accident-and-illness plan in the puppy months, and running the DNA test in those same months, keeps the result and the coverage in the right order.</p>
<p>Wellness add-ons are for the ordinary year: the exam, the vaccines, the ear cytology after a summer of swimming. They are a poor substitute for the illness plan if your real fear is the VCA adult list. Atopy can be the quiet multi-year claim, a dermatology visit and a medication every season, smaller than a bloat surgery and larger than the grooming line. Cataracts and hemangiosarcoma land late, which is why a policy you cancel at age seven abandons the senior chapter AKC's 11-to-13-year expectancy still includes. Ask what happens to the premium as the dog ages, using the ranges on this page as today's quote only. The $2,000 to $10,000 figure already printed below the tables is the scale of a single bad year, and it sits in the same conversation as GDV, a fracture, or a cancer workup, each of which can clear that range on its own.</p>
<p>Insurance sources: <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA</a>, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20100210185346/http://www.offa.org/hipstatbreed.html">OFA</a>, <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/musculoskeletal-system/myopathies-in-dogs-and-cats/exercise-intolerance-and-exercise-induced-collapse-in-dogs">Merck</a>, <a href="https://canine-genetics.umn.edu/canine-genetic-testing/dynamin-1-associated-exercise-induced-collapse">Minnesota</a>, <a href="https://www.akc.org/breeds/labrador_retriever/index.cfm">AKC</a>.</p>
`,
  "commercial/dogs/labrador-retriever/cost-to-own.html": `
<h2>How the printed Labrador bands get spent</h2>
<p>This page already prints the planning envelope: startup $1,000 to $3,000, a year at $1,500 to $4,500, a lifetime at $15,000 to $50,000. The monthly split under that is food $30 to $100, routine veterinary care $20 to $50, insurance $15 to $60, supplies and toys $15 to $50, and grooming $10 to $60. A separate line puts the replacement cycle at $180 to $400 a year, tied to how hard the dog plays and how many people share the house. AKC's life expectancy, 11 to 13 years, is the multiplier. Eleven years at the low annual figure is $16,500, which sits on the bottom of the lifetime band. Thirteen years at the high annual figure is $58,500, which runs past the top of that band. The lifetime range is what you get if most years stay in the middle. One gastric dilatation-volvulus surgery, which VCA lists for adult Labradors, can spend several ordinary years in an afternoon.</p>
<p>Food is the line people underbid. UKC's male in working condition weighs 65 to 80 pounds, a female 55 to 70, and VCA says weight gain worsens joint disease. The $30 end of the monthly food line is a bag of kibble measured for a moderate adult who is already lean. The $100 end is still a dry-food budget. The food page on this site prints $140 to $260 for fresh or raw, which means a household that chooses that style has already left the $30 to $100 row and must add the difference to the annual band. Training treats come out of the same calories and, if you buy them as a separate habit, out of the supplies line. A dog who swims twice a week does not get a second stipend. The coat is water-repellent. The appetite is still the appetite VCA warns about.</p>
<p>Grooming stays cheap if you do it. UKC's coat is short, dense, and close, with a soft undercoat, and a slight wave on the back is allowed. There is no feathering to scissor. VCA asks for a weekly brush, extra brushing when the seasonal shed hits, a bath after mud or a swim, and ear cleaning for dogs that swim. That work fits inside $10 to $60 a month if the bath is your own and the ears are a wipe you already own. It leaves that band when a matted, neglected coat becomes a salon rehabilitation, or when atopy, also on VCA's adult list, turns the bath into a medicated product your veterinarian names. Budget the home routine. Keep the dermatology products as a contingency inside the annual figure, because they can recur every summer for the rest of the 11 to 13 years.</p>
<p>Startup is where the orthopedic paperwork belongs. OFA's table through December 2009 scored 12.0 percent of 208,931 Labrador hips as dysplastic, with 17.6 percent excellent. Those are old cumulative numbers, and they are the reason a puppy price inside the $1,000 to $3,000 startup band should be read next to the parents' hip and elbow reports. The startup band also has to buy a crate long enough for a dog who may stand 24.5 inches with an otter tail to the hock, a bed, a leash, and the first food supply. If the breeder's price consumes the entire startup band, the crate and the first wellness visits have nowhere to sit. Rescue dogs change the purchase number and leave the supply number in place. They also arrive with a history you may not have, which is an argument for pricing insurance, the $15 to $60 monthly line, before the adoption day.</p>
<p>Routine veterinary care at $20 to $50 a month is $240 to $600 a year. That is a wellness rhythm: the exam, the vaccines, the fecal test, the ear check after swim season, the body-condition conversation VCA wants because controlled growth is part of orthopedic risk. Elbow radiographs, a bile-acid or eye workup, and a dental under anesthesia sit outside that monthly rhythm. Those climb into the annual band's upper half or past it. The health-costs page on this site already prints a wellness exam at $50 to $200, blood diagnostics at $200 to $1,000, non-emergency surgery at $500 to $3,000, and emergency care at $1,000 to $5,000 or more. A Labrador owner who only funds the $20 monthly vet line will meet those rows unprepared the year something on VCA's list becomes clinical. Hold the monthly vet line for prevention. Hold a slice of the $1,500 to $4,500 annual figure for the year the elbows or the eyes need more than a vaccine.</p>
<p>Insurance at $15 to $60 a month is $180 to $720 a year, which can live inside the annual band beside food. Bought before a diagnosis, it is the tool aimed at the expensive rows: elbow surgery, bloat, a mast cell tumor, a senior hemangiosarcoma. Bought after the veterinarian has already written the word, it often excludes the word you needed. Exercise-induced collapse, per Merck's 2026 review, has no treatment, so the insurance value there is the first emergency visit and any workup that distinguishes collapse from a seizure or a heart event. The University of Minnesota DNA test is a small, separate fee. It belongs in the startup year, filed next to the hip report, because more than 80 percent of dogs with two copies have collapsed at least once by age four in that lab's summary, so the genotype is known while the dog's hobbies are still being designed.</p>
<p>Supplies at $15 to $50 a month, plus the $180 to $400 yearly replacement cycle, are the Labrador-specific leak. VCA says the retrieving instinct becomes chewing and carrying, and some dogs swallow what they carry. Dumbbells, flirt-pole tugs, and the destroyed leash are ordinary. A foreign-body surgery is the same behavior on a bad afternoon, and it prices like the emergency row, far above the toy budget. Rotate two or three durable toys. Pick up the rocks the dog keeps offering you. The replacement cycle is the honest version of "Labs are hard on gear," and it runs every year of that 11-to-13-year span, which is $2,000 to $5,200 of objects before you count a single veterinary invoice.</p>
<p>Put the year on one sheet. Food at the measured weight, grooming as a weekly brush, insurance chosen against VCA's adult list, and a crate that fits the AKC height. If those four stay inside the printed bands, the lifetime figure stays a plan. If food drifts into an unmeasured extra cup because the dog "looks hungry" after a swim, you pay twice: once at the bowl, and again when the extra weight meets the hips OFA was already scoring as dysplastic in 12 percent of the historical evaluations. The numbers above are the ones this page and the registries already published. A local clinic can quote the imaging. The breed facts tell you which quotes are worth getting in year one.</p>
<p>Cost sources: <a href="https://www.akc.org/breeds/labrador_retriever/index.cfm">AKC</a>, <a href="https://www.ukcdogs.com/labrador-retriever">UKC</a>, <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA</a>, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20100210185346/http://www.offa.org/hipstatbreed.html">OFA</a>, <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/musculoskeletal-system/myopathies-in-dogs-and-cats/exercise-intolerance-and-exercise-induced-collapse-in-dogs">Merck</a>.</p>
`,
  "commercial/dogs/labrador-retriever/first-time-owners.html": `
<h2>The first year with a retrieving house dog</h2>
<p>UKC's character line is the one to believe: friendly, outgoing, eager to please, extremely intelligent, and easy to teach complex tasks. Shyness in a mature dog is a serious fault in that standard. VCA matches the picture from the clinic side, a social dog who usually does well with children and other pets once socialized, with high energy and a moderate tolerance for being alone only after exercise and training. The table on this page already says the time commitment runs from 30 minutes to two hours and more. A first-time owner who pictured a calm couch dog will feel that range in the first week. The dog is doing the job it was built for, carrying objects and asking for the next round.</p>
<p>Size arrives fast. AKC's males finish at 22.5 to 24.5 inches and 65 to 80 pounds, females at 21.5 to 23.5 inches and 55 to 70. The puppy who fits in a lap at ten weeks is the dog who will lean his full chest into a visitor at two years. Teach a sit for greetings while the puppy is still light. VCA's retrieving note, chewing and mouthing and sometimes swallowing objects, is the other first-month project. A soft mouth, which UKC lists as essential, is a retrieve to hand. It is also a puppy who grabs wrists. Trade the wrist for a toy every time, and keep the laundry behind a door. The supplies you replace are the cheap part. A swallowed sock is the expensive one.</p>
<p>Exercise has a ceiling as well as a floor. VCA wants significant daily work: walking, retrieving, swimming, training games. Merck's 2026 review of exercise-induced collapse describes weakness and collapse 5 to 20 minutes into intense exercise, with a rapid recovery and a normal dog in between. The Minnesota lab found the DNM1 variant common enough that roughly 30 to 40 percent of tested Labradors carry one copy. A first dog does not need a five-mile run at ten months. Short retrieves, a sniff walk, and a nap protect growing joints, which VCA already flags for hip dysplasia, elbow dysplasia, and osteochondritis dissecans in the puppy year, and they also avoid the intense-plus-excitement pattern that brings on collapse in dogs with two copies of the variant. Ask the breeder for the parents' EIC results along with hips, elbows, and eyes. The breed page on this site already names those papers. A seller who has never heard of dynamin-1 is handing you the research project.</p>
<p>Home layout matters more than a perfect yard. VCA says Labs do best with outdoor access and that they are adaptable about climate, with a short dense coat that sheds heavily in season. A first-time household can succeed in a house with a fenced yard and a person who is actually home for the midday break. The same household fails when everyone leaves for ten hours and the dog's exercise is a single dark walk. Time alone is a skill you build after the dog is tired, in a crate long enough for the adult size, placed where the family sits. UKC's ears are drop ears. After a muddy walk, wipe them. That thirty-second habit is the ear care VCA singles out for swimming and active Labs, and it starts in week one so the dog does not treat the ear as a fight.</p>
<p>Children and other pets go well in the VCA description when the dog is socialized, and they go badly when the retrieve drive has no rules. A toddler waving food is a moving dummy. A cat who runs is quarry. Introduce both with the Labrador on a leash, fed, and asked for a settle on a bed. Supervise. The standard's friendly temperament is the raw material. The sit at the doorway is the finished dog. Visitors get the same pattern: the dog sits, the guest enters, the retrieve toy comes out only after the greeting is calm. Skipping that because "Labs love everyone" is how a 70-pound chest knocks someone down in month four, when the puppy weight becomes the AKC adult weight.</p>
<p>Food and weight are a first-year skill, and they get harder to repair once the dog is grown. VCA says controlled growth and weight management matter because orthopedic disease gets worse when the dog is heavy. Feed the amount that keeps a waist, using the sex-specific AKC weights as the adult target, and keep treats inside that amount. A first-time owner who tops the bowl every time the dog stares will meet the joint list early. The cost page prints food at $30 to $100 a month for the ordinary plan. Stay there until a veterinarian changes the diet. Atopy and other medical diets are a response to a diagnosis, which shows up in the adult chapter of VCA's handout, after the first year has already taught you what this dog's ribs feel like.</p>
<p>Plan the people, too. A groomer is optional for this coat if you brush weekly and bathe after mud. A trainer is useful in the first month because the breed learns quickly and will also learn the wrong game quickly. A boarding kennel or a sitter needs a note that this dog eats non-food items and may be a swimmer, so socks and small toys are picked up and the pool gate stays shut. Insurance, priced before any limp is in the record, sits on the cost page at $15 to $60 a month. Buy it while the puppy is still a blank chart. The first year is busy because the dog is built that way. It is also the year the paperwork, the crate, and the retrieve rules are cheap compared with the adult who never learned them.</p>
<p>Starter sources: <a href="https://www.ukcdogs.com/labrador-retriever">UKC</a>, <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA</a>, <a href="https://www.akc.org/breeds/labrador_retriever/index.cfm">AKC</a>, <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/musculoskeletal-system/myopathies-in-dogs-and-cats/exercise-intolerance-and-exercise-induced-collapse-in-dogs">Merck</a>.</p>
`,
  "commercial/dogs/labrador-retriever/health-costs.html": `
<h2>VCA's age list, priced against this page</h2>
<p>The rows already on this page are the price language: a wellness exam $50 to $200, a minor illness $100 to $500, blood diagnostics $200 to $1,000, non-emergency surgery $500 to $3,000, emergency care $1,000 to $5,000 or more, and a specialist referral $500 to $3,000 or more. VCA sorts Labrador disease by age, and the bills follow that sort. From birth to 12 months the handout lists hip dysplasia, elbow dysplasia, osteochondritis dissecans, retinal dysplasia, and tricuspid valve dysplasia. Those are imaging, ophthalmology, and cardiology, which means the specialist row and the diagnostic row, during the same year you are also paying wellness exams. Controlled growth, which VCA names as part of orthopedic risk, is the cheap intervention: a lean puppy costs kibble. A dysplastic hip that becomes clinical costs the surgery row.</p>
<p>OFA's archived statistics, January 1974 through December 2009, for breeds with at least 100 evaluations, put the Labrador Retriever at 208,931 hip evaluations, rank 82, with 17.6 percent excellent and 12.0 percent dysplastic. Bring that table to a breeder visit as history, then ask for the parents' own certificates. A puppy evaluation, if your veterinarian wants baseline films once the dog is old enough, lands in the diagnostic band. It is money spent to know. It is smaller than the surgical band you meet if the dog is already lame. Elbow dysplasia and osteochondritis dissecans live next door. They are the reason "hips only" is an incomplete packet. A front-end limp in a growing Labrador is an elbow conversation until the radiographs say otherwise, and the visit is the specialist row if your general practice sends you out.</p>
<p>Adult years, one through eight on the VCA card, add exercise-induced collapse, epilepsy, atopic dermatitis, progressive retinal dysplasia, gastric dilatation-volvulus, and mast cell tumors. Collapse has its own paperwork. The Minnesota Canine Genetics Lab calls EIC the most common reason young healthy Labradors go down with exercise or excitement, ties it to a recessive DNM1 mutation, and reports that in tested groups about 3 to 13 percent of dogs have two copies while 30 to 40 percent carry one. More than 80 percent of the two-copy dogs have collapsed at least once by age four. The DNA test itself is a small ticket beside these rows. The first collapse can look like an emergency, because Merck describes weakness progressing to ataxia and collapse over 5 to 20 minutes, with a kneecap reflex that may disappear, then a rapid recovery and a normal dog afterward. That first episode can bill as the emergency row while everyone rules out a seizure, heat stress, or a heart problem. Merck notes that dogs with one copy, and dogs without this mutation, can still collapse, so a clear test closes one door and leaves the rest of the workup standing. Afterward there is no treatment in the Merck review. You stop doing the intense work. The cost becomes behavior design, which is cheaper than a second emergency visit.</p>
<p>Epilepsy, if it is the diagnosis, is a medication year stacked on wellness, often inside the minor-illness to diagnostic range at the start and then a monthly drug cost for a long time. Atopy is the skin version of that long bill. VCA lists allergic skin disease in adults. Cytology, a diet trial, and a drug your veterinarian picks can outlast any single surgery in total dollars because they recur. Track them against the annual budget on the cost page, $1,500 to $4,500, because a "minor" skin dog can quietly use the top of that band every summer. Progressive retinal dysplasia and the puppy-year retinal dysplasia are eye appointments, the ophthalmologist, and they belong on the calendar before a dog loses vision and you are buying furniture bumpers. Ask for the eye certificate the breed page already tells buyers to request, then repeat the exam when the dog is middle-aged. Cataracts arrive on VCA's senior list, eight years and older, which is a later surgery conversation inside the $500 to $3,000 non-emergency band or beyond it if the clinic's cataract fee sits higher. Get the local number. The row is the shape of the bill.</p>
<p>Gastric dilatation-volvulus is the adult emergency VCA names. A deep-chested dog, restless, with a swelling abdomen after a meal, is the case that clears the $1,000 to $5,000 emergency line and can keep going. The health-cost plan for bloat is twofold: know the route to the emergency clinic before the night you need it, and keep the dog's weight in the AKC range so the joints stay out of the recovery. Mast cell tumors and, in seniors, hemangiosarcoma are the cancer chapters. VCA tells owners to have new lumps, appetite changes, and low energy seen, and it puts cancer screening in the aging plan. A lump that is checked while it is small may be a minor procedure. A belly bleed from a splenic mass is the emergency row plus the specialist row. AKC's 11-to-13-year expectancy means many dogs reach the senior list. Fund wellness visits all the way through it. The $50 to $200 exam is how the expensive diseases get named early enough to have a choice.</p>
<p>Tricuspid valve dysplasia is the puppy heart item on VCA's list. A murmur at the vaccine visit should become an echocardiogram, specialist pricing, before anyone assumes the puppy is only excited. Intact dogs and breeding dogs have additional papers, and a pet owner still wants the result on the parents. None of these ranges are a quote from your clinic. They are the bands this page already uses, lined up with the conditions VCA, OFA, Merck, and the Minnesota lab actually publish for the breed. The useful budget is a wellness habit plus an emergency number you have already saved, with the DNA test and the orthopedic films done while the startup money is still the topic.</p>
<p>Health-cost sources: <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA</a>, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20100210185346/http://www.offa.org/hipstatbreed.html">OFA</a>, <a href="https://canine-genetics.umn.edu/canine-genetic-testing/dynamin-1-associated-exercise-induced-collapse">Minnesota</a>, <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/musculoskeletal-system/myopathies-in-dogs-and-cats/exercise-intolerance-and-exercise-induced-collapse-in-dogs">Merck</a>, <a href="https://www.akc.org/breeds/labrador_retriever/index.cfm">AKC</a>.</p>
`,
  "commercial/dogs/labrador-retriever/vs-labradoodle.html": `
<h2>Same height on paper, different coats and clubs</h2>
<p>A standard Australian Labradoodle, in the size classes published by the Australian Labradoodle Association, stands 21 to 24 inches and weighs 21 to 30 kilograms. AKC's Labrador male stands 22.5 to 24.5 inches and weighs 65 to 80 pounds, and the female stands 21.5 to 23.5 inches and 55 to 70 pounds. The inches overlap. The life that goes with those inches does not. Medium doodles on that Australian chart are 17 to 20 inches and 13 to 20 kilograms, and miniatures are 14 to 16 inches and 7 to 13 kilograms. Comparing "a Labradoodle" to a Labrador without the size class compares a 15-inch dog to a 24-inch dog and then acts surprised at the food bill. This page's monthly bands, $120 to $280 for the Labrador and $100 to $320 for the Labradoodle, can both be true because the doodle band has to cover three body sizes and a grooming schedule the Labrador coat never needs.</p>
<p>The Labrador coat is a tool. UKC describes a short, dense, water-repellent double coat, outer hair close and straight, a slight wave on the back allowed, undercoat soft and dense. The otter tail wears the same thick coat and is never feathered. VCA says the upkeep is a weekly brush, a heavier brush when the seasonal shed arrives, a bath after swimming or mud, and ear cleaning if the dog swims. Shedding is the housekeeping. Clippers are for the rare matted patch, and they are how you ruin the jacket a water dog is supposed to wear. The Australian association's coat page describes a different animal: fleece, soft and wavy or curly, wool that feels like lamb, and a hair coat that sheds, has odor, shows up in early generations, and is called a major fault. Desired length is 4 to 6 inches. A single coat is the aim. A double coat is listed as a fault. Someone who wants the Labrador's wash-and-wear shed will be unhappy with a fleece coat that mats if it is missed for a month. Someone who wants a low-shed house will be unhappy with VCA's seasonal Labrador blow.</p>
<p>Registration is the other split. UKC recognized the Labrador Retriever in 1947. AKC places it in the sporting group and publishes the height, weight, and 11-to-13-year expectancy used above. The Australian Labradoodle Association of America states that the Australian Labradoodle currently lacks AKC recognition, keeps its own standard, and notes that OFA has given the dog a separate breed code so health records can be filed apart from Labrador and Poodle records. ALAA also says a consumer DNA breed test is a weak stand-in for a multigenerational pedigree. If a seller's entire proof is a printout from a breed-guessing kit, you still do not know which size class, which coat, or which parents you are buying.</p>
<p>Generation labels change the dog in the crate. ALAA defines an F1 as a purebred Poodle crossed to a purebred Labrador, with mixed results, often moderate to low shedding and a sparse-hair or fleece coat. An F1B is that dog bred back to a Poodle. An Australian Labradoodle, in the same FAQ, carries Labrador, Poodle, and Cocker Spaniel, American or English. A multigenerational dog is an Australian Labradoodle bred to another one. ALAA's own purebred label waits for four consecutive like-to-like matings, and the FAQ notes that AKC's foundation service looks for four generations of like-to-like as well. An F1 still has a Labrador parent. That parent can be asked for the Labrador packet: hips, elbows, eyes, and the DNM1 result the Minnesota lab uses for exercise-induced collapse. A multigenerational dog's Labrador ancestor may be several generations back. The tests that matter are the ones run on the parents in front of you, filed under the doodle's own OFA code if the clinic uses it, plus whatever the doodle clubs your seller actually belongs to require. A Labrador DNA panel on a great-grandparent is a story. It is the wrong document for the puppy's own hips.</p>
<p>Health lists do not transfer one-for-one. VCA's Labrador handout is specific: hip and elbow dysplasia, osteochondritis dissecans, retinal dysplasia, and tricuspid valve dysplasia in the puppy; exercise-induced collapse, epilepsy, atopy, progressive retinal dysplasia, bloat, and mast cell tumors in adults; hemangiosarcoma and cataracts in seniors. OFA's historical hip table, through December 2009, found 12.0 percent of 208,931 Labrador evaluations dysplastic. There is no matching public sentence for "Labradoodles as a class" in those two sources, and inventing one would treat three size classes and several generation types as one breed. What you can say is mechanical. An F1 can inherit a DNM1 copy from the Labrador parent, so ask for that parent's result. A 21-to-24-inch standard doodle can carry enough chest to make bloat worth knowing about, and a 14-inch miniature is a different orthopedic problem. Atopy shows up in Poodles and in Labradors. Skin cost follows the individual dog, which is why the doodle's monthly band on this page stretches to $320. Grooming is the reliable extra. A 4-to-6-inch fleece or wool coat on a six-to-eight-week salon cycle will spend money the Labrador's $10 to $60 grooming line, printed on the Labrador cost page, often never sees.</p>
<p>Exercise is where owners get hurt by the word "doodle." VCA wants a Labrador to have significant daily work and describes a dog who will chew and retrieve if that work is missing. UKC evaluates the Labrador as a gun dog and faults movement when it interferes with the work. A standard doodle with a Labrador parent often has a similar engine and a coat that overheats sooner if you run it in full fleece at noon. Merck's collapse window, 5 to 20 minutes of intense work, applies to Labradors with the variant and, Merck notes, can also appear in dogs who do not have the variant. Heat plus frantic fetch is a poor plan for either coat. A miniature doodle may finish the same walk looking fresher, and may still need the grooming hour the Labrador spends swimming. Match the exercise to the size class you measured, then match the afternoon to the coat you can actually dry.</p>
<p>Money on this page's comparison row should be read with those differences in hand. The Labrador's $120 to $280 month is food for a 55-to-80-pound dog, a short-coat brush, and preventive care, with insurance as the hedge against VCA's surgical list. The doodle's $100 to $320 month moves up when the coat is wool and the dog is standard, and it can sit lower when the dog is miniature and you learn to bathe at home. Neither band includes a foreign-body surgery if the Labrador habit of swallowing toys survived in an F1. Neither band is a quote. They are the ranges this page already shows. The decision is whether you want UKC's otter-tailed, shedding retriever with an AKC height you can look up, or a dog whose size, coat, and pedigree you have to reconstruct from ALAA's generation rules and a stack of parent tests. Both can be good family dogs. They are a match only after you have seen the parents, the coat type, and the height class written down.</p>
<p>Comparison sources: <a href="https://www.akc.org/breeds/labrador_retriever/index.cfm">AKC</a>, <a href="https://www.ukcdogs.com/labrador-retriever">UKC</a>, <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA</a>, <a href="https://alaa-labradoodles.com/">ALAA</a>, <a href="https://australianlabradoodleassoc.org.au/labradoodle-breed-standards/">ALA sizes</a>, <a href="https://australianlabradoodleassoc.org.au/labradoodle-coat-types/">ALA coats</a>.</p>
`,
  "commercial/dogs/labrador-retriever/vs-lagotto-romagnolo.html": `
<h2>Two water dogs, one of them now a truffle dog</h2>
<p>UKC's Labrador is a gun dog whose ancestor, the Saint Johns Retriever, came off fishing boats and was kept for retrieving from water. The modern standard still judges the dog as a worker: square or slightly long, short dense double coat, otter tail no longer than the hock, soft mouth, good nose. AKC's Lagotto Romagnolo page starts in the same element and leaves it. The breed goes back at least to Renaissance Italy as a waterfowl retriever in the marshlands of Ravenna. Lago means lake. AKC also calls the dog the Romagna Water Dog, then describes the modern job as truffle hunting, and states that the Lagotto is the only purebred dog recognized as a specialized truffle searcher. Both dogs have a nose and a history in water. One is still scored as a retriever. The other is scored as a curly dog who hunts a smell underground.</p>
<p>The bodies make the budget obvious. AKC puts Labrador males at 22.5 to 24.5 inches and 65 to 80 pounds, females at 21.5 to 23.5 inches and 55 to 70 pounds, with a life expectancy of 11 to 13 years. The same club puts Lagotto males at 17 to 19 inches and 28.5 to 35 pounds, females at 16 to 18 inches and 24 to 31 pounds, with a life expectancy of 15 to 17 years. A Lagotto male at the top of the standard weighs about half of a Labrador male at the bottom. Food follows the ribs. This page prints $120 to $280 a month for the Labrador and $100 to $320 for the Lagotto. The overlap is real, and the reason is coat and care, because the smaller dog can spend more at the groomer than the larger dog spends at the feed store. AKC's Lagotto life line is also four years longer at the top, 17 against the Labrador's 13, so a monthly cost that looks similar becomes a larger lifetime pile if you simply multiply. Seventeen years at $200 a month is a different promise from thirteen years at the same number.</p>
<p>Coat care is the daily difference you will feel. The Labrador jacket, in the UKC standard, lies close, sheds, and wants the weekly brush and the post-swim bath VCA describes. Seasonal shedding is heavy. The house dust is Labrador-colored for a few weeks twice a year, and the rest of the time a brush and a washable cover cope. The Lagotto coat, on the AKC care page, is rough-looking and waterproof, thick tight curls over the whole body except the head, with a beard, eyebrows, and whiskers. AKC says the curls behave more like hair than fur, shedding is minimal, little tufts can still appear, and the coat needs regular trimming because some dogs mat more than others. Ears get a weekly check. A Labrador owner who skips a month of grooming has a dusty house. A Lagotto owner who skips a month can have a felted jacket that hurts. The grooming line is a subscription for the Lagotto and a brush in a drawer for the Labrador, which is how the smaller dog's monthly band can reach $320.</p>
<p>Exercise looks similar on a breed-selector form and splits in the yard. VCA wants the Labrador to swim, retrieve, and train, and warns that a bored dog chews, carries, and sometimes swallows objects. The Minnesota lab's DNM1 work puts a ceiling on frantic intensity: dogs with two copies of the collapse variant, a few percent up to about 13 percent of tested Labradors depending on the group, can go down 5 to 20 minutes into hard work, in Merck's timing, and then look fine an hour later. A Lagotto, in AKC's exercise section, needs an active life with both mental work and physical work, does well at scenting and dock diving, and is described as needing the family nearby. The truffle job is nose work with a walk attached. The Labrador job is a marked retrieve with a swim attached. A household that hikes and hides food will suit the Lagotto. A household that throws a bumper into a pond will suit the Labrador. Swapping them produces a curly dog who is asked to be a retriever, or a Labrador who is asked to be calm after one lap of the block.</p>
<p>Health packets are different documents. For the Labrador, take hips, elbows, eyes, and a DNM1 result, then read VCA's list: hip and elbow dysplasia, osteochondritis dissecans, retinal problems, tricuspid valve dysplasia, epilepsy, atopy, bloat, mast cell tumors, and later hemangiosarcoma and cataracts. OFA's 1974-to-2009 hip file holds 208,931 Labrador evaluations and a 12.0 percent dysplastic rate, with 17.6 percent excellent. For the Lagotto, AKC's parent-club list is patella evaluation, hip evaluation, an ophthalmologist exam, a DNA test for benign familial juvenile epilepsy, and a DNA test for Lagotto storage disease. The same AKC health note says a responsible breeder also screens for elbow dysplasia, and that the breed is generally a healthy one with those conditions on the radar. Juvenile epilepsy and a storage disease are the Lagotto-specific DNA questions. They are the wrong questions to skip because you already asked a Labrador breeder about exercise-induced collapse. Bring the form that matches the puppy. A Lagotto puppy's clear EIC test does not answer BFJE, and a Labrador puppy's clear BFJE test is a test for a disease the Labrador form was not built around.</p>
<p>Training tone follows the job. UKC says the Labrador is eager to please and easy to teach complex tasks, with shyness in a mature dog treated as a serious fault. AKC calls the Lagotto alert, intelligent, and lively, good at agility and obedience, and strong in scent work. Both can live in a family. The Labrador's failure mode is enthusiasm with a 70-pound chest and a mouth that collects household objects. The Lagotto's failure mode is a brilliant nose and a coat that seized into mats while everyone was busy admiring the beard. Socialize both. Provide a crate. Check ears on both, because the Labrador's drop ear holds swim water and the Lagotto's ear hides under curls. Then pick the mess you prefer: hair on the sofa for a decade, or a grooming appointment on the calendar for the longer Lagotto lifespan.</p>
<p>The monthly comparison on this page is a fair start and a poor ending. Use it after you have weighed the dog you will actually feed, 28 to 35 pounds against 65 to 80 for the males at the standard tops and bottoms, and after you have priced a trim schedule against a weekly brush. Add the years. AKC gives the Lagotto 15 to 17 and the Labrador 11 to 13, so the curly dog may be in the budget after the retriever's chapter has closed. Add the emergency you are insuring. Bloat and elbow surgery sit on the Labrador list. Storage disease and juvenile epilepsy sit on the Lagotto list. A family that wants a pond dog who carries a bumper, sheds, and lives in the low teens of years is describing the UKC Labrador. A family that wants a smaller curly dog, a truffle-style nose, minimal shed, and a longer life is describing the AKC Lagotto. The breeds share a waterfowl past. The dogs in the yard diverged when one kept the otter tail and the other grew the beard.</p>
<p>Pairing sources: <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/lagotto-romagnolo">AKC Lagotto</a>, <a href="https://www.akc.org/breeds/labrador_retriever/index.cfm">AKC Lab</a>, <a href="https://www.ukcdogs.com/labrador-retriever">UKC</a>, <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/labrador-retriever">VCA</a>, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20100210185346/http://www.offa.org/hipstatbreed.html">OFA</a>.</p>
`,
};
